Histamine Intolerance / Suspected MCAS
Integrative & Functional medicine
Pruritic hives/flushing, Severe gastrointestinal distress after aged/fermented foods, Extreme tachycardia/palpitations, Symptomatic improvement on low-histamine diet

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IFM-017
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe systemic flare involving generalized urticaria, extreme tachycardia, and profuse diarrhea requiring urgent assessment to rule out acute anaphylaxis or acute severe gastrointestinal infection.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated a patient experiencing rapid-onset facial flushing, full-body intensely itchy hives, racing heart, and severe abdominal cramping immediately following a meal. Urgent on-site physical and hemodynamic assessment was strictly required to exclude life-threatening IgE-mediated anaphylaxis with airway compromise, preventing catastrophic respiratory or cardiovascular collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My entire body is on fire with hives, my heart is pounding out of my chest, and I have severe stomach cramps after eating leftover lasagna."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden and highly severe exacerbation of symptoms beginning 45 minutes ago, immediately following the consumption of highly specific aged/leftover distinct foods (reheated lasagna with aged cheese). The patient developed sudden, distinct severe intensely pruritic generalized hives (urticaria) and prominent facial flushing. This was rapidly followed by severe, distinct intense abdominal cramping, profound severe sudden diarrhoea, and distinct profound bloating. The patient also complains of unexplained distinct sudden extreme tachycardia, profound palpitations, and distinct severe orthostatic dizziness. Historically, the patient suffers from recurrent, distinct severe intense sinus congestion and profuse rhinorrhoea entirely unassociated with an active viral infection, alongside extreme distinct sleep-onset insomnia and profound generalized fatigue.
Functional Status: Highly distressed, actively scratching, frequently using the bathroom.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of progression to severe hemodynamic compromise if symptoms escalate.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/68 | HR: 118 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is overtly flushed, diaphoretic, anxious, and actively scratching arms and legs. Breathing is rapid but unlabored.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals widespread, intensely erythematous, raised, and highly pruritic wheals (urticaria) across the trunk and extremities, with marked facial flushing. Crucially, respiratory exam reveals clear air entry bilaterally, no wheeze, no stridor, and no obvious uvular or tongue/lip angioedema (airway is definitively secure). Cardiovascular exam confirms significant sinus tachycardia without murmurs. Abdominal exam shows hyperactive bowel sounds and diffuse cramping tenderness, but no rigidity or rebound peritonism. The presentation is classic for a severe histamine storm: pruritic hives/flushing, severe GI distress after aged foods, and extreme tachycardia. Confirmation requires clinical improvement on a low-histamine diet and elevated serum tryptase levels during a flare.
Environmental Observations: Empty food containers of reheated, aged foods visible in the kitchen. No obvious environmental allergens (e.g., new pets).
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient took one over-the-counter Loratadine 10mg one hour ago with minimal effect. No known IgE-mediated food allergies.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Histamine Intolerance / Suspected MCAS - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute IgE-Mediated Anaphylaxis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of respiratory compromise (no wheeze, stridor, or lip/tongue swelling) and maintenance of normotension despite the tachycardia.

Carcinoid Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Symptoms are distinctly triggered by specific high-histamine foods rather than unprovoked or alcohol-triggered, and absence of chronic, persistent right-sided heart murmurs.

Acute Food Poisoning (Bacterial Toxin) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Prominent presence of systemic hives and facial flushing, which are uncharacteristic for simple staphylococcal or bacillus food poisoning.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe, multi-systemic hypersensitivity reaction featuring generalized urticaria, extreme tachycardia, and profuse diarrhea. Telephone triage correctly identified the critical, time-sensitive need to evaluate for impending anaphylactic shock and airway occlusion. Immediate on-site clinical assessment was strictly necessary to directly visualize the oropharynx, auscultate the lungs for bronchospasm, and assess blood pressure. The physical exclusion of airway angioedema, wheeze, and profound hypotension confirmed the patient was not in immediate, life-threatening anaphylaxis requiring IM Adrenaline. However, the severity of the generalized hives, the debilitating abdominal cramping, and the extreme physical distress dictated that delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a distinct, unacceptable risk of progression to shock, severe dehydration, and immense suffering. This urgent intervention unequivocally satisfies MBS Items 585-600 criteria.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Promethazine 25mg orally stat to aggressively block H1 receptors and provide sedation/nausea relief. Advised strict avoidance of all leftover, fermented, or aged foods. Recommend routine GP follow-up for a comprehensive allergy/immunology referral and measurement of serum tryptase/DAO enzymes.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe histamine overflow, initiate strict dietary and environmental controls. Action: Implement a rigid protocol of eating only freshly cooked meals; absolutely no leftovers or aged cheeses for the next 14 days. Consequence: Eliminating exogenous dietary histamine physically prevents the "bucket" from overflowing, directly rewarding the patient with the cessation of hives and severe abdominal cramping. Action: Take a second-generation H1 antihistamine (e.g., Cetirizine) daily precisely at 0800 hours. Consequence: Proactive, scheduled receptor blockade explicitly prevents the chemical binding of endogenous histamine, rewarding the patient with stabilized heart rate and reduced facial flushing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated currently.
DME/Safety (Optional): None indicated.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48-72 hours to assess baseline and discuss low-histamine dietary protocols.

